Chapter: WebMuxHeapTest
  cites Net chapter WebServer
  cites Net chapter TcpTransport
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The os-backlog entry for the web mux says a server spends a receive buffer
 plus the mux records for every connection it ever accepts, and carries no
 NUMBER. This arm is that number, and it is the measurement rather than the
 repair.

 WHAT IS MEASURED IS WHAT `web-mux-accept` ALLOCATES. That function builds a
 fresh listen transport per accepted connection and pushes a `WebConn` into
 `conns`, and the transport is where the bytes are: `fresh-listen-transport`
 ends in `transport-new-with-cap sess2 serve-recv-buf-cap`, 65536. The
 function itself carries `[Network.Read, Network.Write]` and cannot run
 without a wire, but the allocating half of it is pure and is called here
 directly, which is why this needs no network and no peer.

 THE TRANSPORTS ARE RETAINED IN A LIST, as the mux retains them, for two
 reasons: nothing can eliminate a construction whose result is still
 reachable, and `list-push` extends in place with a doubling capacity, so the
 list's own growth is part of what a connection costs and is measured here
 rather than reasoned about.

 THE THREE COUNTS ARE THE POINT, not any one of them. A per-connection figure
 taken at n=1 includes the list's first allocation and every fixed cost the
 first call pays; the figure at 16 is what a server actually spends per
 connection. Reporting one number would have been a claim the arm could not
 support.

 `__heap-save` answers an Integer and the delta between two marks is the
 bytes allocated between them, which is the instrument `net-recv-heap`
 already uses.

Section: The Measurement

  build : Integer, List TcpTransportState -> List TcpTransportState
  build (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else build (n - 1) (list-push acc (fresh-listen-transport 8080))

 The length guard is not decoration: it is what makes the list REACHABLE at
 the second mark, and a run that answered -1 would be reporting that the
 transports were not built and the delta measures nothing.

  per-conn : Integer -> Integer
  per-conn (n) =
    let a = __heap-save
    in let l = build n []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in if list-length l < n then 0 - 1 else (b - a) / n

  built : Integer -> Integer
  built (n) = list-length (build n [])

 WHAT THE THREE COUNTS ABOVE CANNOT SEE. Every one of them RETAINS its
 transports, so each answers "what does a live connection cost". The mux's
 own question is different: `web-mux-remove` rebuilds a list and calls no
 `__heap-restore`, and the only restore in the chapter is in
 `web-mux-loop`'s empty-poll window, which `WebServer.codex` says in its own
 prose the sweep runs outside of. If a closed connection returns nothing,
 the cost is per connection EVER ACCEPTED and not per concurrent one, and
 the two readings differ by the lifetime of the server.

 `sum-bases` builds the same transports and keeps NO reference to them: it
 accumulates one per transport whose `recv-base` is positive, so the answer
 is exactly n and every transport must be constructed to produce it. A
 folded or eliminated construction cannot answer n.

  sum-bases : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-bases (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else let t = fresh-listen-transport 8080
    in sum-bases (n - 1) (acc + (if t.recv-base > 0 then 1 else 0))

  per-conn-discarded : Integer -> Integer
  per-conn-discarded (n) =
    let a = __heap-save
    in let k = sum-bases n 0
    in let b = __heap-save
    in if k < n then 0 - 1 else (b - a) / n

 THE CONTROL, and the arm is worth nothing without it. This measures the
 identical construction inside a `__heap-save` / `__heap-restore` window,
 which is the one reclamation the memory model has. It must answer about
 zero. An instrument that reported 65,536 here would be reporting that it
 cannot read a reclaimed heap at all, and the discarded figure above would
 mean nothing (L-FALSIF).

  per-conn-restored : Integer -> Integer
  per-conn-restored (n) =
    let a = __heap-save
    in let k = sum-bases n 0
    in let r = __heap-restore a
    in let b = __heap-save
    in if k < n then 0 - 1 else (b - a) / n

 WHAT THE POOL COSTS. `rebind-listen-transport` is `fresh-listen-transport`
 with `recv-base` and `recv-cap` carried over from a closed connection's
 transport, so the 65,536-byte `__heap-advance` does not happen and what
 remains is the session and the record. The donor is built BEFORE the first
 mark, so its own buffer is not in the figure. The difference between this
 and `per-conn-discarded` is the buffer, and it is the whole point of the
 pool.

  sum-bases-recycled : TcpTransportState, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-bases-recycled (t) (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else let t2 = rebind-listen-transport t 8080
    in sum-bases-recycled t (n - 1) (acc + (if t2.recv-base > 0 then 1 else 0))

  per-conn-recycled : Integer -> Integer
  per-conn-recycled (n) =
    let donor = fresh-listen-transport 8080
    in let a = __heap-save
    in let k = sum-bases-recycled donor n 0
    in let b = __heap-save
    in if k < n then 0 - 1 else (b - a) / n

 WHAT A CAP ON `free` WOULD BUY, which is the open question the pool left.
 Capping the pool drops the excess transports, and a dropped transport's
 buffer came from `__heap-advance` inside `transport-new-with-cap`. The heap
 is a single bump pointer whose only reclamation is `__heap-restore` to a
 mark, and the mark that precedes a pooled transport also precedes every live
 connection, so no close path can take it. `drop-delta` measures what the
 drop returns: it holds n transports, drops one through the mux's own
 `pool-drop-first`, and reports the heap delta across the drop. A NEGATIVE
 answer would mean bytes came back and a cap is worth pricing. A
 non-negative answer means a cap returns nothing and only guarantees a
 re-mint at `discarded` above.

  drop-delta : Integer -> Integer
  drop-delta (n) =
    let pool = build n []
    in let a = __heap-save
    in let kept = pool-drop-first pool 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in if list-length kept /= (n - 1) then 0 - 1 else b - a

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap per-conn n=1 " & show (per-conn 1))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap per-conn n=4 " & show (per-conn 4))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap per-conn n=16 " & show (per-conn 16))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap built n=16 " & show (built 16))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap discarded n=16 " & show (per-conn-discarded 16))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap restored n=16 " & show (per-conn-restored 16))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap recycled n=16 " & show (per-conn-recycled 16))
    print-line-uni ("web-mux-heap drop-delta n=16 " & show (drop-delta 16))
  end
